{"@odata.context":"$metadata#Directions/$entity","booleans":{},"counters":{},"entity_id":"en-019d9565-e481-7ca3-9063-d3eb8ab786b2","entity_type":"Direction","events":[{"action":"Created","from_status":"","params":{},"timestamp":"2026-04-16T08:26:16.067750Z","to_status":"Proposed"},{"action":"Propose","from_status":"Proposed","params":{"counterfactual_summary":"If this direction is wrong, generic benchmarks and ordinary unit-test gates will be sufficient to rank coding agents, and teams will not need custom replay suites tied to their own repositories and maintenance history.","grounding":"[]","observation_ids":"["en-019d9565-3c5d-7de0-9b2b-9af8eb2cb604","en-019d9565-83d4-7d40-906d-8d97fcc95c68"]","projection_id":"en-019d9564-d1e4-7b90-a9c7-b851c26d4945","proposer_agent_id":"aj-019d9564-d22b-7f62-bb80-b97571738c86","reasoning":"Evaluation/testing direction: the highest-leverage investment in the next 180 days will be repo-specific agent evaluation harnesses that combine deterministic tests with task-replay scoring. Practitioners are learning that benchmark wins do not transfer cleanly into production repositories; what matters is whether an agent can resolve the classes of work that actually consume engineering time, such as fixing flaky tests, updating APIs, migrating configs, or repairing broken CI. That pushes evaluation closer to historical issue traces, captured incidents, and bounded task budgets rather than abstract coding benchmarks alone.

